### 1. Why wasn't a `Pages` + `blocks` architecture the default first instinct?

When AI coding assistants evaluate a CMS setup without an explicit architectural mandate, two factors usually bias the initial implementation toward **isolated per-section collections & globals**:

1. **"Entity-First" Bias vs "Page-Builder" Bias**:
   - AI models typically see distinct entities (Articles, Resources, Team Members, Values, Country Portals) and translate them 1:1 into collections or globals.
   - For marketing pages, unless instructed to build a flexible visual/page-builder CMS, AI models default to keeping page layouts in code (`page.tsx`) with hardcoded composition, assuming only dynamic lists (news, team, testimonials) and site-wide copy (header/footer) need CMS controls.

2. **Conservative Scope & Type Safety**:
   - Building a full page-builder system requires defining a comprehensive block registry (`Hero`, `TwoColumnFeature`, `CardGrid`, `PricingTierGrid`, `Timeline`, etc.), rendering components (`<Blocks />`), and managing polymorphic block props.
   - Without an explicit requirement for **"editors must be able to add, remove, and reorder full page sections on any page"**, an AI model will choose the least presumptuous architecture (specific content collections + code-driven layout).

